course: dictionary
lesson: 2327
title: Crowd: a group, a verb, and a squeeze
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can be a noun (a thing) or a verb (an action), and the sentence tells you which. «A crowd cheered when the goal went in.» Here crowd is a noun: a large group of people together in one place.
explain: «The fans crowd around the gate before the match.» Now crowd is a verb: the fans are gathering closely together. Ask yourself whether it is a thing or something people are doing, then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: The words around a word leave clues. «When a crowd pushes, move to the side towards space» tells you that a crowd squeezing too tightly can be dangerous, so you should move towards open space where you can breathe. Crowd can also mean to stand too close to someone, and the feeling of being crowded is not a nice one.
fact: A crowd is a large group of people together in one place.
fact: To crowd means to gather closely together, or to fill a place so it is very full.
fact: To crowd someone means to stand too close to them.
fact: Crowd can be a noun («a crowd of fans») or a verb («the fans crowd in»).
fact: If you get lost in a crowd, find a staff member or police officer, and agree a meeting place with your family before you go out.
exercise: «A crowd cheered when the goal went in.» What kind of word is crowd here? | A question word | A verb: to gather closely together | *A noun: a large group of people | An adjective: very loud
exercise: «When a crowd pushes, move to the side towards space.» Why should you move towards space? | *A tight crush can hurt people, and space is safer | Space is where the crowd keeps its coats | So you can find the snack stall more quickly | Because the match is played in space
exercise: Ella gets lost in a crowd at a market. What should she do? | *Find a staff member or police officer | Stand still and let the crowd push her | Crowd closely up to a stranger for safety | Run to the nearest parade
test: «The shoppers crowded the market stalls.» What does crowded mean here? | Went home early | Left the market quietly | *Filled the place so it was very full | Sold their goods cheaply
test: «The man kept crowding him in the queue.» What does crowding him mean? | Giving him a present | *Standing too close to him | Asking him a question | Waving at him from far away
exam: Which sentence uses crowd as a verb? | The crowd went wild at the final whistle | *The fans crowd into the stadium before kick-off | Crowds gather at the parade every year | A crowd of 40,000 fans filled the stadium
